  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1233 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nAn upper trough is currently over the Great Lakes with weak ridging \nover the Northern and Central Plains. An upper shortwave trough is \nsliding across the Desert Southwest towards the Central/Southern \nRockies. At the surface, expansive high pressure is situated over \nthe Northern Plains with surface ridging extending down to the \nSouthern Plains. In our area, light easterly/northeasterly winds \ncontinue with highs in the 40s. \n\nAs we move into tonight and Tuesday morning, cloud cover will start \nto increase as the aforementioned upper shortwave trough shifts into \nthe Plains. Minimal impacts are expected with this system, as the \ncolumn will remain dry over the eastern half of the state. Light \nprecipitation is expected across far western KS with snow flurries \nlikely, though little to no accumulation is expected with much of \nthat area seeing under a half inch. This trough will bring another \ncold snap to the region as below normal temperatures for this time \nof year are progged for Tuesday and Tuesday night with highs in the \nlower 40s and low temperatures near 20 degrees.  \n\nThis cold snap will be short-lived as temperatures will start to \nrebound after Wednesday as upper ridging builds in with thickness \nresponding accordingly and increasing. By Friday, highs will return \nto the 50s with upper 50s forecast for Saturday. Similarly, low \ntemperatures are expected to rise back into the 30s. Mostly dry \nconditions are likely through this period as well.",
